Three hiring mistakes we keep seeing

Good morning, operators. This week we went back through forty conversations with founders who made their first five hires in the last year.

Hire for the job you have next quarter

The most expensive hiring mistake we see is hiring for the company you are today. By the time a new operator is fully ramped, usually three to four months, the job has changed. Write the role description for the business you expect to be next quarter, and ask every candidate how they handled a job that changed underneath them.

Stop running interviews without a scorecard

If your team cannot write down what a great answer sounds like before the interview, the interview is measuring charm. A one-page scorecard with four outcomes and the evidence you want for each takes an hour to write and saves weeks of debate afterwards. Share it with candidates too; the good ones prepare better.

Your first operations hire is not an assistant

Founders often hire their first operator as a person to take tasks off their plate. The hires that work are the ones given a system to own: billing, onboarding, vendor management. Give them the outcome and the budget, then get out of the way. Ownership is what makes a good operator stay.

